---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Prose from "../components/Prose.astro";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import Cloud from "reicon/icons/Cloud";
import Rocket from "reicon/icons/Rocket";

const installCommand = "curl -fsSL https://cdn.coollabs.io/coolify/install.sh | sudo bash";

const faq = [
  {
    question: "Do I need to know Docker to use Coolify?",
    answer:
      "No. Nixpacks or Railpack can build most repositories without a Dockerfile, and Coolify creates and runs the containers. Dockerfiles and Compose files work too.",
  },
  {
    question: "What size VPS do I need for Coolify?",
    answer:
      "The documented minimum is 2 CPU cores, 2 GB RAM, and 10 GB disk. Plan for more as you add apps, databases, and builds.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can Coolify run on a 1 GB VPS?",
    answer:
      "It can, but that is below the recommended minimum. The docs suggest 1 core and 1 GB only if cost is a concern, and they say to watch resource use during builds.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I use my own domain with free HTTPS?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Point DNS at your server, add the domain with `https://`, and the proxy requests and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is Coolify really free?",
    answer:
      "Self-hosted Coolify is free and open source, with the same features as Coolify Cloud. Coolify Cloud is an optional paid service that runs the Coolify instance for you.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Deploy Apps on Your VPS Without Managing Docker | Coolify"
  canonicalUrl="https://coolify.io/deploy-apps-on-your-own-vps"
  description="Install Coolify on your VPS with one command. It handles Docker builds, HTTPS, and backups. See what a small VPS can run."
>
  <section class="px-4">
    <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
    <h1 class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance text-fg md:text-5xl">
      How do I deploy apps on my own VPS without managing Docker by hand<span class="text-warning">?</span>
    </h1>
  </section>

  <article class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-3xl px-4 text-left">
    <Prose>
      <p class="text-base md:text-lg">
        <strong>Short answer:</strong> Install Coolify, an open-source alternative to Heroku, Vercel, and Netlify, and
        let it manage Docker for you. Connect a Git repository and push. Coolify builds the app, routes traffic, and
        requests a free Let's Encrypt certificate, so you do not need a Dockerfile or <code>docker</code> commands
        unless you want them.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <div class="card mt-4 py-3 px-4">
      <code class="block overflow-x-auto font-mono text-sm whitespace-nowrap text-fg select-all">
        <span class="pr-2 text-fg-faint select-none">$</span>{installCommand}
      </code>
    </div>

    <Prose class="mt-4">
      <h2>Install</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Start with a fresh Linux server you can reach over SSH. Supported distributions are listed in the
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/start-with-self-hosted">self-hosted install guide</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Run the command above as root, or with sudo. The installer adds Docker Engine when your distribution supports
          it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Open <code>http://&lt;your-server-ip&gt;:8000</code> and create the admin account immediately. The first
          person to register becomes the admin.
        </li>
        <li>
          Back up <code>/data/coolify/source/.env</code>. You need that file to restore Coolify.
        </li>
        <li>
          The <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/deploy-your-first-app">first-app guide</a> deploys
          <code>nginx:alpine</code> on port 80, on a generated sslip.io address. You do not need a Git repo for that
          test.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The <a href="/self-hosted">self-hosted page</a> says Coolify is ready on your server in under a minute. The wait
        is longer when Docker still has to be installed. If you do not want to run the dashboard,
        <a href="/cloud">Coolify Cloud</a> hosts it and you connect your servers.
      </p>

      <h2>What a small VPS can run</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A standard install is three containers: <code>coolify</code> (dashboard and API), <code>coolify-db</code>
          (PostgreSQL for Coolify's own state), and <code>coolify-redis</code>. Each server also runs a reverse proxy.
          <code>coolify-sentinel</code> monitoring is optional. Coolify talks to servers over SSH and does not install a
          permanent agent.
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/core/how-coolify-works">How Coolify works</a>.
        </li>
        <li>Documented minimum: 2 CPU cores, 2 GB RAM, and 10 GB disk (amd64 or arm64).</li>
        <li>
          The docs say Coolify can start smaller, for example 1 CPU core, 512 MB RAM, and 4 GB disk, but that is not
          recommended. If cost is the limit, start with 1 core and 1 GB RAM and upgrade as you grow. Builds on that
          same server can make it unresponsive. Move builds to a
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/core/infrastructure/servers/build-servers">build server</a> or to CI.
        </li>
        <li>
          One right-sized server is easier to run than several. Add servers when load or availability needs it.
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/core/infrastructure/scaling/overview">Scaling overview</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Push to deploy</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Connect a public repository, a deploy key, or a GitHub App. GitLab, Bitbucket, and Gitea work too.
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/applications/sources/overview">Sources</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Point DNS at the server and enter the domain with <code>https://</code>. Traefik or Caddy requests and renews
          the certificate. Open ports 80 and 443.
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/core/infrastructure/servers/firewall">Firewall</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Nixpacks or Railpack can build most repositories. A Dockerfile, a Compose file, a static site, or a prebuilt
          image also works. Coolify
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/core/docker-and-containers">generates the Compose configuration</a> for apps
          and databases.
        </li>
        <li>
          Containers that are already running keep serving traffic if the Coolify dashboard is down. You still own OS
          updates, the firewall, SSH, and a tested restore.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Many small tools cover git push deploys</h2>
      <p>Coolify also gives you:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A <a href="/services">one-click services catalog</a> (360+ services).
        </li>
        <li>
          Scheduled database backups to S3-compatible storage, including PostgreSQL. See
          <a href="/self-host-postgres-with-backups">self-hosting Postgres with backups</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          More than one server from one dashboard. Coolify connects over SSH and installs or checks Docker Engine and
          Compose during validation.
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/core/infrastructure/servers/overview">Servers</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/applications/deployments/preview-deployments">Preview deployments</a> for pull
          requests on GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, and Gitea.
        </li>
        <li>
          Open source under the Apache-2.0 license. The
          <a href="https://github.com/coollabsio/coolify">GitHub repository</a> started in 2021 and still publishes
          <a href="https://github.com/coollabsio/coolify/releases">releases</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For a sourced comparison with Dokploy, CapRover, Dokku, and Kamal, see
        <a href="/compare/coolify-vs-dokploy-vs-caprover-vs-dokku-vs-kamal">Coolify vs Dokploy vs CapRover vs Dokku vs Kamal</a>.
        Short buyer answers are on <a href="/free-heroku-alternative">free Heroku alternatives</a> and
        <a href="/netlify-alternative">Netlify alternatives</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Self-hosted or Cloud</h2>
      <p>
        Both have the
        <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/core/selfhosted-cloud-comparison">same features</a>. Your apps run on your
        servers either way.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Self-hosted:</strong> free and open source (Apache-2.0). You install, update, and back up the Coolify
          instance.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Coolify Cloud:</strong> the Coolify team hosts the dashboard at app.coolify.io. It starts at $5 per
          month for 2 servers, plus $3 per month for each additional server. Pricing can change. See
          <a href="/pricing">pricing</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>
    </Prose>

    <div class="mt-4">
      <Faq items={faq} />
    </div>

    <section class="card mt-14 p-6 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-fg">Get started<span class="text-warning">.</span></h2>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-xl text-sm text-fg-dim">
        Got a VPS? Run the installer and deploy your first app. If you would rather not run the dashboard, try Coolify
        Cloud.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
        <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/start-with-self-hosted" class="btn btn-primary">
          <Icon icon={Rocket} class="size-4" />
          Start with self-hosted
        </a>
        <a href="/pricing" class="btn btn-neutral">
          <Icon icon={Cloud} class="size-4" />
          Try Coolify Cloud
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
